drm/esp32s31-lcd: stop using GDMA for the damage copy - it is the slowest
of the three engines, and it was pre-empting the other two

The port tree is gitignored, so this is the whole modified file rather
than a diff. Copy it back over
linux-71-port/drivers/gpu/drm/espressif/esp32s31-lcd.c before building.

The change is one word: `static bool gdma_copy = true;` becomes
`static bool gdma_copy;`. The comment above it carries the numbers.

WHAT WAS WRONG. esp32s31_lcd_copy_one() dispatches like this:

	if (!scaled && esp32s31_lcd_gdma_rows(...))
		return;
	... CPU below ppa_min_bytes ...
	... PPA above ...

GDMA is tried FIRST for every unscaled copy and returns on success, so it
handled all of them at every size and the carefully measured CPU/PPA
crossover below it never applied to an unscaled surface at all.

HOW IT WAS FOUND. lvdesk was measured at 42% of all cycles during a
320x200 prboom timedemo (prboom 53.7%, everything else 3.9%, 0.12% idle),
with ~60% of lvdesk's own time in the kernel rather than in its pixel
loops. Timing the stages from userspace (lvdesk LVPROF=1, which brackets
the DIRTYFB ioctl - a synchronous commit, so the ioctl time IS the
driver-side cost) put a 31.8 ms frame at:

	DIRTYFB      10.5 ms    33% of the frame
	expansion     5.1 ms    16%
	flush total  15.8 ms    50%

THE MEASUREMENT. Forcing each engine with the existing module parameters,
per frame:

	engine        DIRTYFB    composited frames    Doom fps
	GDMA          10.6 ms          3600             31.0
	CPU memcpy     7.9 ms          4000               -
	PPA            7.8 ms          4200             31.7

Reproduced across two independent runs (10.51/7.76, then 10.61/7.80), and
confirmed a third time on a fresh boot with the new default compiled in:
7.79 ms, flush 13.09 ms, 32.1 fps. Painting verified by hardware
screenshot on a separate untimed run.

CPU and PPA are within 2% of each other at Doom's damage size (128,000
bytes, just under the 131072 crossover), so simply removing GDMA captures
the win without forcing PPA everywhere - which would be wrong for small
rectangles, where the table above ppa_min_bytes has the CPU at 0.03 ms
against the engine's 0.43 ms for 32x32.

WHY GDMA IS SLOWER. Almost certainly the completion wait rather than the
transfer, which is the same shape as the defer_copy finding in the same
file: a kworker round trip cost 7.4 ms and was charged to the client's
ioctl, so it "appears as fixed per-commit overhead rather than as pixel
cost, which is exactly how it hid". This path already carries a 50 ms
bound for a stall that once spun for five seconds.

CAVEAT. Measured on one workload. gdma_copy stays a parameter; set
gdma_copy=Y to put it back.
